 "name": "Xiamen Island Streets: Would You Like to Cycle Here? (0–100)",
 "description": "Method replication: participants see 20 streets on Xiamen Island, each as two street views from the same point in opposite directions, and answer \"Would you like to cycle on this street?\" on a 0–100 slider. The images are not the paper's stimuli (43 Baidu street views fetched for this template, compass 0° and 180° views side by side with the car roof cropped, 2019–2021 captures; tunnels and dark views removed).\n\nOriginal study: Hu, Huang, Fang, Luo, Zhang & Wang (2025), \"How Do Street Landscapes Influence Cycling Preferences? Revealing Nonlinear and Interaction Effects Using Interpretable Machine Learning: A Case Study of Xiamen Island\", Land 14(11), 2253. 30 volunteers (university students and staff aged 20–50) viewed pairs of Baidu street views from the same sampling point on a rating platform and scored their willingness to cycle from 0 to 100; a model then predicted preferences for 8,440 points and SHAP showed the nonlinear effects of greenery, sky and other street elements.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) the paper does not say which two of its four directions formed a pair; this template shows 0° and 180°; (2) 20 randomly drawn streets per participant; (3) the prediction model is not part of the template.",
